(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-05"] = {"9.1":{"s":"Probability starts with a random experiment, whose result cannot be predicted but whose possible results are known. The set of all possible results is the sample space. Any part of it is an event. A single result is a simple event. Two events are mutually exclusive if they cannot happen together, and exhaustive if one of them must happen. The complement of an event A is the event that A does not happen.","w":["Mutually exclusive means no common result. Independent is a different idea and does not mean the same."]},"9.2":{"s":"The probability of an event is a number between 0 and 1 that measures how likely the event is. An impossible event has probability 0 and a sure event has probability 1. For equally likely results, it is the number of results favourable to the event divided by the total number of results in the sample space. The probability of the whole sample space is 1.","f":[{"x":"0 <= P(A) <= 1","n":"Probability of any event A lies between 0 and 1.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"},{"x":"P(S) = 1","n":"The probability of the whole sample space S is 1.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"}],"w":["A probability can never be negative or more than 1; if you get such an answer, recheck the count."]},"9.3":{"s":"The addition theorem gives the probability that at least one of two events happens. Add the two probabilities, then subtract the probability that both happen, because that overlap was counted twice. If the events are mutually exclusive, the overlap is zero and the probabilities simply add. In words: A or B means the union of the two events.","f":[{"x":"P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A ∩ B)","n":"At least one of A, B happens. For mutually exclusive events P(A ∩ B) = 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"}],"w":["Do not forget to subtract P(A ∩ B) unless the events are mutually exclusive."]},"9.4":{"s":"Conditional probability is the probability of an event when we already know that another event has happened. Knowing that B has occurred shrinks the sample space to B. So the probability of A given B is the probability that both happen divided by the probability of B. It is defined only when P(B) is not zero. Reversing the roles of A and B gives the other form.","f":[{"x":"P(A/B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B)","n":"Probability of A given that B has occurred; P(B) is not zero.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"}],"w":["P(A/B) and P(B/A) are different numbers. Check which event is the condition: it goes in the denominator."]},"9.5":{"s":"The multiplication theorem gives the probability that two events both happen: the probability of the first times the probability of the second given the first. If the events are independent, the occurrence of one does not change the chance of the other, so the probabilities simply multiply. If A and B are independent, then A and the complement of B are also independent.","f":[{"x":"P(A ∩ B) = P(A)*P(B/A)","n":"Both events A and B happen.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"},{"x":"P(A ∩ B) = P(A)*P(B)","n":"Only for independent events A and B.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"}],"w":["Use the simple product P(A)P(B) only when the events are independent, such as separate coin tosses.","Drawing without replacement makes the events dependent: the second probability changes."]},"9.6":{"s":"Bayes' theorem reverses a conditional probability. Suppose the events E1, E2, up to En are mutually exclusive and together cover the whole sample space. If an event A has happened, the probability that it came through a particular Ei is found by dividing the probability of reaching A through that Ei by the total probability of A over all the Ei.","f":[{"x":"P(E_i/A) = P(E_i)*P(A/E_i)/(P(E_1)*P(A/E_1) + ... + P(E_n)*P(A/E_n))","n":"E_1, ... E_n are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, with P(A) greater than 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"}],"w":["The denominator is the total probability of A: add the product for every Ei, not just the one asked for.","Draw a tree diagram first; the events E must be mutually exclusive and exhaustive."]},"9.7":{"s":"Odds compare the chance of an event happening to the chance of it not happening, written as a ratio. Odds in favour of A are the ratio of the favourable results to the unfavourable results. Odds against A are the same ratio turned round. Odds can be converted to probability and back, which is how problems on odds are solved.","w":["Odds 3:2 in favour mean 3 favourable out of 5 total outcomes, not a probability of 3/2."]}};
